## Research and provenance protocol

- Read the local field map, FieldSpecs and media search reports as leads, not authority. Trace each material historical claim to a primary record, archive or scholarship; cite the precise source and distinguish source-stated fact, author's interpretation, teaching inference and unresolved question. A case does not establish a field-wide consensus or measured teaching effect. Visually similar forms do not establish influence.
- Look across material, motion, cinema and media circulation, and feminist, postcolonial/decolonial, queer, labor, capitalist and Ibero-American work where specific sources support it. Avoid treating Europe or an undifferentiated “non-West” as the universal frame. A missing source stays a gap. Consider who controls the body, movement and representation; who performs labor; who is absent; whose knowledge is universalized; and what counterevidence would change the reading.
- Research stage/film/fashion translations and distinguish documented influence, exchange, quotation, appropriation and analogy. Read scholarship critically rather than adopting promotional language. In particular, abjection is an analytical proposal, not a synonym for shocking imagery; do not infer a butoh lineage from appearance.
- Follow `PROVENANCE-LAW.mdc` and `ground-with-athanor-ahmes`: scoped vector discovery → actual complete Ahmes node and page → Chicago resolver → `evaluator_safe=yes`. Vector snippets are never evidence. Keep incomplete or unsafe metadata as `[BIBLIO-GAP]` with the precise reason; a source-checked external record in the private ledger is not automatically an Ahmes-safe classroom citation. If unavailable, label the spine not release-ready and record attempted scope and failure; do not claim an unsearched corpus. No injection into shared infrastructure without authorization. For later promotion follow `AI-EVIDENCE-HARNESS.md` and `curriculum-publication-head`: audited hashes, source manifest, evaluator-safe decisions, gaps, targets and owner release in `grounding/PROFIELD-SYNC.json` before promotion. No automatic publishing.

## Media, rights and accessibility

- Discovery ≠ acceptance. A slide uses only a Profield asset accepted into the reviewed collection or a course-generated accessible diagram under project rules. For each candidate record canonical asset/source, creator, date, exact photo/video license and evidence URL, credit, intended use, permitted copy/display/embed/crop/remix actions, alt text or accurate transcript, and fallback. Mark unknown and contradictory rights as rejected or pending, never guess. Review underlying work, performance, recording and photograph separately; confirm captions and jurisdictional rights. An asset cannot graduate without human acceptance.
- Public availability, linking, framing, downloading, and adapting are distinct. No LIFE iframe, screenshot, thumbnail, download or derivative without current explicit authorization for the specific use; use a separate ordinary source link only if terms and project rules permit, otherwise instructor navigates directly. A course-created diagram is the visual fallback. Do not reuse source-site images merely because an article is linked.
- Check every moving image for uploader authority, exact work and segment, captions/transcript, motion-accessible static/text alternative and permission for the intended embedding. Source-led visual comparisons need non-visual equivalents. Cite media in the private ledger; do not expose manifests, local paths, identifiers, confidence or unresolved rights in student content.

